UNIT-II] Exception, Threading and Resource Management

C# Exception

Definition -An exception is a runtime error which results in abnormal
termination of program.

A C# exception is a response to an exceptional circumstance that arises while a
program is running, such as an attempt to divide by zero.

Exception handling is used to make sure that the program flow is continued
even if there is a runtime error. Exceptions are the objects that are thrown while
encountering any runtime error.

Tf the exceptions are not handled, then C# will produce an exception message and
will terminate the program abnormally, this is where exception handling comes into
the picture.

The following example shows program execution with an exception. As you can see,
in the below code, we are dividing an integer number by 0 which is not possible in
mathematics. So, it will throw the DivideByZeroException in this case. The
statements which are present before the exception-causing statement i.e. before
c=a/b; 1s executed and the statements which are present after the exception-causing
statement will not be executed.

using System;
namespace ExceptionHandlingDemo

{

class Program

static void Main(string[] args)

{

int a = 20;
int b =0;
int c;

Console. WriteLine("A VALUE =" + a);
Console. WriteLine("B VALUE =" + b);

c=a/b;

Console. WriteLine("C VALUE =" + c);
Console.ReadKey();

}

}

